WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.360
Have you ever heard someone say they're

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.830
"blind as a bat"?

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:11.780
Well that expression is a bit misguided.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:15.170
During the day, some kinds of bats can

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:17.510
see almost as well as humans. But when

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:20.029
the sun goes down, nocturnal bats rely on

00:00:20.029 --> 00:00:21.890
their ears more than their eyes to navigate.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:24.589
This special navigation process

00:00:24.589 --> 00:00:27.710
is called echolocation. In echolocation,

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:30.019
bats use a combination of the voice,

00:00:30.019 --> 00:00:32.870
ears, and echoes to create a perceptual

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:35.210
map that allows them to "see" in the dark.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:37.160
You might be wondering where an echo

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.830
comes from. An echo is the reflection of

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:42.560
sound reaching a listener. Sound is a

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.570
vibration that ripples away from its

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.150
source in waves. The rate at which this

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:49.970
vibration occurs is called its frequency.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.940
Sound waves at high frequencies are

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.550
high-pitched. Humans can hear sounds at

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.590
frequencies between 20 and 20,000

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.510
waves per second and don't

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.190
perceive pitches outside that range.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.950
Bats, on the other hand, can hear and

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:05.930
produce sound at frequencies over

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:09.290
100,000 waves per second- way beyond the

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.470
range of human hearing. Here, we've slowed

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:15.590
down a bat's chirp, so you can hear it!

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:18.500
This chrip is the first step in the

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.170
process of bat echolocation, the primary

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:23.060
sound that the bat will use to detect

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:26.060
information about its surroundings.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.520
The bat listens as the chirp leaves its body

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.190
and bounces off its surroundings.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:32.960
When the sound returns to the bat, the

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.060
cartilage of the outer ear funnels the

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.030
sound waves into the ear canal. The waves

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.729
then reach the eardrum, which vibrates in

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:43.040
response. These vibrations are

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.020
transferred further into the ear to the

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.000
basilar membrane, which runs the length

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.729
of the cochlea. If you uncoil the cochlea

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:51.890
and the basilar membrane, they would look

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:53.000
like this.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.220
Each area of the membrane moves in

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.770
response to a particular frequency. Then,

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.290
the cochlea converts these vibrations

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.299
into nerve impulses for the brain to

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:05.930
interpret. The bad echolocate so fast

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.610
that it can track down a dinner of

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.830
insects moving around in complete

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:12.500
darkness. Special muscles in its throat

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.660
make it possible for the bat to chirp

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:17.810
160 to 190 times

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.239
per second! Because it receives so much

00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:22.220
information at once, it has to

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.319
differentiate relevant information from

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:27.500
signal clutter. To do this, the bat emits

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:29.599
chirps of varying frequency patterns,

00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:31.520
each of which echoes off objects

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.890
differently depending on the object's size,

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:37.430
shape and distance from the bat. Then, by

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.980
analyzing resulting echo intensities, it

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.050
creates a mental map of its surrounding

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:45.260
physical space. When looking for prey, the

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.660
bat first sends out low-frequency chirps

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:50.540
over a wide range. Llarger objects will

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.730
reflect more intense echoes, but it's

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:55.280
often the smaller objects, like insects

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.920
that the bat is targeting. Once a bat

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.260
receives low-intensity echoes from small

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.599
potential prey, it will remit a high

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:05.569
frequency chirp directed toward it. When

00:03:05.569 --> 00:03:07.610
both high-and low-frequency echoes

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:10.190
return to the bat, it is able to ignore

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.319
the now-irrelevant, low-frequency ways

00:03:12.319 --> 00:03:14.209
that reflect from the trees or rocks

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:17.209
that surround its meal. To gauge how far

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:20.060
away a given object is, the bat analyzes

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.070
the time delay between when it hears its

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:24.320
chirp and perceives the resulting echo.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.810
Directional information comes from

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.820
comparing information the bat receives

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.850
in each ear. For example, let's imagine

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:34.190
that a bat's chirp echoes back from an

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:37.280
insect to this side of the bat. Since

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.010
this ear is closer to the insect, it

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:41.989
will receive the sound before the other ear.

00:03:41.989 --> 00:03:44.239
By interpreting the difference

00:03:44.239 --> 00:03:45.920
between when it receives the echo in

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.200
each ear, the bat can guide itself

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.200
towards the insect. Bat are only one

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.030
type of animal that use echolocation.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:57.139
killer whales, dolphins, porpoises and

00:03:57.139 --> 00:04:00.260
some birds echolocate as well. Scientists

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.450
who developed sonar and radar got their

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.340
ideas from the study of echolocation in

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:07.609
bats and whales. The U.S. Navy and other

00:04:07.609 --> 00:04:09.139
research groups are especially

00:04:09.139 --> 00:04:11.569
interested in echolocation because it

00:04:11.569 --> 00:04:14.569
could lead to even more technology!

00:04:14.569 --> 00:04:16.519
Maybe now you'll think twice the next time

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:23.260
someone says "blind as a bat!"

